        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "How-To Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Polymeric Sand Installation Mistakes",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/polymeric-sand-installation-mistakes/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What are the most common polymeric sand mistakes?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Installing into damp joints, leaving sand on the paver faces, watering too hard or too much, filling joints too shallow, and sealing before the sand has fully cured. Most of these look fine on the day and only reveal themselves weeks later as haze, crumbling joints or washouts.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does polymeric sand leave a haze on pavers?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Because sand or binder was left on the paver surface when the joints were watered. Any polymeric sand sitting in surface texture or a chamfer cures there and bonds to the stone. Sweeping plus a leaf blower held at a shallow angle before watering is what prevents it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How deep should polymeric sand joints be?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most manufacturers want a minimum of about an inch to an inch and a half of depth. Shallower fills do not develop enough mechanical grip on the paver faces and lift out as strips. Remove old compacted sand to reach that depth rather than sweeping a thin layer over the top.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much water does polymeric sand need?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Enough to saturate the joint through its full depth, applied in several light passes on a shower setting rather than one heavy soaking. Watch the runoff: clear water means the binder is staying in the joints, milky water means it is being carried onto the paver faces where it will cure as haze.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you fix polymeric sand that was installed wrong?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Joint problems generally yes — failed sand can be removed and reinstalled properly. Surface problems are harder, because cured polymeric haze bonds to the stone and mechanical removal risks damaging the paver face. That asymmetry is why the surface-clearing step deserves disproportionate care.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long before you can walk on polymeric sand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most products want 24 hours before foot traffic and 24 to 48 hours before vehicles, with longer in cool or humid weather when curing slows. Traffic before the joints have set breaks the developing bond and leaves joints that never reach full strength.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
