        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#prep-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Preparation Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#installation-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Installation Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#watering-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Watering Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#after-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. After Installation</a>
          <a href="#fixing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Fixing These Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#sequence" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. The Correct Sequence</a>
          <a href="#cost-of-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. What Each Mistake Costs</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Polymeric sand is unforgiving in a specific way: nearly every mistake looks fine on the day you make it and only reveals itself weeks later, as a haze on the pavers, joints that crumble underfoot, or sand that leaves in the first heavy rain.
        </p>

        <div id="prep-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Preparation Mistakes: Where Most Failures Begin
          </h2>

          <p>
            The polymers in polymeric sand activate with water and bind the grains into a flexible solid. Everything that goes wrong is ultimately about that reaction happening at the wrong time, in the wrong place, or not at all — and preparation determines all three.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Installing Into Damp Joints</h3>

          <p>
            This is the single most common error. Any moisture in the joints starts activating the polymer the moment the sand touches it, which means it begins setting while you are still sweeping. The result is partially cured clumps buried in the joint that never bond to anything, and a surface layer that looks finished but has nothing underneath it. The pavers and the joints must be bone dry before a single bag is opened, which in practice means a full dry day after any rain or washing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Joints That Are Too Shallow</h3>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand needs depth to develop strength. Fill a joint half an inch deep and the cured plug has almost no mechanical grip on the paver faces beside it, so it lifts out as a strip. Most manufacturers want a minimum depth of about an inch to an inch and a half, and joints wider than the product is rated for need a different sand entirely. Sweeping in a thin surface layer over old, compacted sand is the version of this mistake people make most often.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Working in the Wrong Weather</h3>

          <p>
            You need a genuinely dry window, and that means checking the forecast for at least 24 hours after installation rather than just the afternoon you are working. Rain on freshly installed, uncured polymeric sand washes it out of the joints and across the pavers, where it then cures into a haze that is genuinely difficult to remove. Cold is the other constraint: most products need temperatures above roughly 50°F for the polymers to cure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Installation Mistakes: Filling and Sweeping
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leaving Sand on the Paver Surface</h3>

          <p>
            This is the mistake that produces the visible, permanent damage. Polymeric sand left anywhere on the paver face — in a surface texture, in a chamfer, in a low spot — cures there and bonds to the stone. The haze that results is not dirt and it will not wash off. Sweep the surface thoroughly, then go over it again with a leaf blower on a low setting held at a shallow angle, which lifts residue out of the texture that a broom leaves behind.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Overfilling the Joints</h3>

          <p>
            Joints should be filled to roughly an eighth of an inch below the paver surface, not level with it and certainly not proud. Sand filled to the top has nowhere to go when it is wetted and settles, and any that sits above the chamfer is effectively sand on the paver face — which brings you straight back to the haze problem.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Skipping the Compaction and Top-Up</h3>

          <p>
            Sweeping sand in and walking away leaves air voids in the joint. The correct sequence is to sweep in, vibrate or tamp so the sand settles and consolidates, then top up the joints that have dropped, and repeat until they stay at the right level. Skipping this gives you joints that look full and are hollow, and they fail from within.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Using the Wrong Product for the Joint Width</h3>

          <p>
            Polymeric sands are rated for a joint width range, typically something like a quarter inch up to an inch or more for standard products, with dedicated wide-joint formulations beyond that. Using a narrow-joint product in a wide joint gives you a plug with insufficient binder for its volume, and it cracks and crumbles. Check the bag against your actual joints before buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="watering-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Watering Mistakes: The Step Everyone Gets Wrong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Watering activates the polymer, and it is where a careful installation is most often undone in the final ten minutes.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Mistake</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What Happens</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What To Do Instead</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Too much water at once</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Washes sand out and floats binder onto pavers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Multiple light passes, letting each soak in</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Too little water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polymer activates only at the surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water until the joint is saturated through its depth</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">High-pressure nozzle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Blasts sand out of the joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Shower or fan setting, held high</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Watering unevenly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Some joints cure, others stay loose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Work systematically in sections</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Not clearing standing water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cures into a film on the paver face</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Squeegee or blow puddles off promptly</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The correct technique is a shower setting, several light passes rather than one soaking, and attention to what the water is doing on the surface between passes. If you see white or milky water running across the pavers, you are pushing binder out of the joints and onto the stone — stop, let it drain, and reduce the flow.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Detail That Prevents Haze</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Watch the runoff, not the joints.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Clear water running off the edge means the polymer is staying where it belongs. Milky or white runoff means binder is being carried onto the paver faces, where it will cure into a haze. This is the single most useful thing to watch during the watering step, and almost nobody does.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="after-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Mistakes Made After the Sand Is Down
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Walking or Driving on It Too Soon</h3>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand needs time undisturbed. Most products want 24 hours before foot traffic and 24 to 48 hours before vehicles, and longer in cool or humid conditions when curing slows. Traffic before the joints have set disturbs the surface, breaks the developing bond, and produces joints that never reach full strength.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealing Too Soon</h3>

          <p>
            This is expensive when it goes wrong. Polymeric sand continues curing well past the point where it feels hard, and sealing over sand that is still releasing moisture traps that moisture under the sealer film, producing a cloudy, hazy finish across the whole installation. The widely recommended wait is at least 30 days after installation before any sealer goes down.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pressure Washing the New Joints</h3>

          <p>
            A pressure washer will strip cured polymeric sand out of joints without much effort, and on new joints it removes them entirely. Clean new paver installations with a hose and a broom. If pressure washing is genuinely necessary later, use a wide fan at low pressure and expect to top up joints afterwards.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Assuming It Is Permanent</h3>

          <p>
            Polymeric sand is durable but it is a maintenance material, not a permanent one. Joints erode gradually, particularly at edges, in drainage paths and where a snow shovel or plough passes. Inspecting annually and topping up the joints that have dropped keeps a patio in good condition indefinitely; ignoring it until joints are visibly empty means a full redo.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What You Can Fix and What You Cannot
          </h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Problem</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fixable?</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Approach</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Haze on paver faces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sometimes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polymeric sand haze remover, promptly; harder once fully cured</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joints washed out</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove remaining loose sand, re-install into dry joints</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joints never hardened</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove entirely and re-install; more water will not fix it</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sand cracking and crumbling</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually a depth or product-width problem; remove and redo properly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealed over uncured sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Difficult</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sealer stripping, then allow full cure before resealing</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sand cured on the surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Difficult</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mechanical removal risks damaging the paver face</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The pattern is worth noting: joint problems are almost always fixable because the sand can be removed and replaced, while surface problems are the ones that persist. That asymmetry is the argument for spending disproportionate care on the two steps that protect the paver faces — clearing every trace of sand off the surface before watering, and watching the runoff while you water.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are looking at joints that have failed rather than a hazed surface, the honest approach is removal and reinstallation rather than patching. Adding fresh polymeric sand on top of a failed layer gives you a thin cured crust over loose material underneath, and it fails again within a season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sequence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Correct Sequence, Start to Finish
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-4 my-8">
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">1</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Confirm the joints are dry and deep enough</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bone dry throughout, and at least an inch to an inch and a half of depth available. Remove old sand to reach it if necessary.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">2</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Check the product against your joint width</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Standard products cover a specific range. Wide joints need a wide-joint formulation, not more of the standard one.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">3</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Check the forecast for 24 hours</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">You need a dry window after installation and temperatures above roughly 50°F for the polymers to cure.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">4</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Sweep sand into the joints in stages</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Fill, then vibrate or tamp to consolidate, then top up. Repeat until joints hold at about an eighth of an inch below the surface.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">5</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clear the surface completely</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Broom thoroughly, then a leaf blower at a shallow angle to lift residue out of the paver texture. This step prevents the haze.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">6</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Water in light passes</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Shower setting, several passes, letting each soak in. Watch the runoff — milky water means binder is moving onto the pavers.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">7</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Clear any standing water</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Puddles on the surface cure into a film. Squeegee or blow them off before they sit.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex gap-4 items-start bg-gray-50 border border-gray-100 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black flex-shrink-0">8</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 m-0 mb-1">Keep traffic off, then wait to seal</p>
                <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">24 hours before foot traffic, 24 to 48 before vehicles, and at least 30 days before any sealer.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost-of-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Each Mistake Actually Costs to Put Right
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not all of these errors are equal. Some are a wasted afternoon; others mean living with a permanently marked patio. Knowing which is which tells you where to concentrate.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Mistake</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Severity</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost to Fix</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sand left on paver faces</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Haze remover, or permanent if fully cured</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealed over uncured sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strip the sealer, wait, reseal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Installed into damp joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove and reinstall the sand</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Joints too shallow</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excavate deeper and refill</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Over-watered, binder washed out</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Remove and reinstall, plus haze cleanup</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under-watered</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Re-water if same day, otherwise reinstall</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Walked on too early</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually recovers; top up disturbed areas</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The two high-severity rows share a characteristic: both damage the paver surface rather than the joint. Joints can always be dug out and redone, which makes joint mistakes recoverable inconveniences. Surface damage bonds to the stone, and mechanical removal risks taking the paver face with it.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the argument for spending your care unevenly. The steps that protect the surface — clearing every trace of sand before watering, watching the runoff, and waiting the full 30 days before sealing — deserve more attention than the steps that only affect joint quality, because those are the ones you cannot walk back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing Into Damp Joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Any moisture present when the sand goes in starts the polymer reacting immediately, so it begins curing while you are still sweeping. You end up with partially set clumps buried in the joint that bond to nothing. Joints and pavers must be bone dry, which usually means a full dry day after rain or washing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Sand on the Paver Faces</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polymeric sand left anywhere on the stone cures there and bonds to it, and the resulting haze is not dirt and will not wash off. Broom the surface thoroughly and then go over it with a leaf blower held at a shallow angle to lift residue out of the surface texture before watering.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Watering Too Hard or Too Much</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A high-pressure nozzle blasts sand out of the joints, and too much water at once floats binder onto the paver faces where it cures as haze. Use a shower setting in several light passes and watch the runoff — clear water is fine, milky water means you are moving binder onto the stone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling Joints Too Shallow</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polymeric sand needs depth to develop mechanical grip on the paver faces. A half-inch fill lifts out as a strip; most products want an inch to an inch and a half minimum. Sweeping a thin layer over old compacted sand is the most common version of this error.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Before the Sand Has Fully Cured</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Polymeric sand keeps curing long after it feels hard, and sealing over it traps that moisture under the film, producing a cloudy finish across the whole patio. The widely recommended wait is at least 30 days between installing polymeric sand and applying any paver sealer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the most common polymeric sand mistakes?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Installing into damp joints, leaving sand on the paver faces, watering too hard or too much, filling joints too shallow, and sealing before the sand has fully cured. Most of these look fine on the day and only reveal themselves weeks later as haze, crumbling joints or washouts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does polymeric sand leave a haze on pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because sand or binder was left on the paver surface when the joints were watered. Any polymeric sand sitting in surface texture or a chamfer cures there and bonds to the stone. Sweeping plus a leaf blower held at a shallow angle before watering is what prevents it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep should polymeric sand joints be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most manufacturers want a minimum of about an inch to an inch and a half of depth. Shallower fills do not develop enough mechanical grip on the paver faces and lift out as strips. Remove old compacted sand to reach that depth rather than sweeping a thin layer over the top.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much water does polymeric sand need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Enough to saturate the joint through its full depth, applied in several light passes on a shower setting rather than one heavy soaking. Watch the runoff: clear water means the binder is staying in the joints, milky water means it is being carried onto the paver faces where it will cure as haze.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you fix polymeric sand that was installed wrong?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Joint problems generally yes — failed sand can be removed and reinstalled properly. Surface problems are harder, because cured polymeric haze bonds to the stone and mechanical removal risks damaging the paver face. That asymmetry is why the surface-clearing step deserves disproportionate care.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before you can walk on polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most products want 24 hours before foot traffic and 24 to 48 hours before vehicles, with longer in cool or humid weather when curing slows. Traffic before the joints have set breaks the developing bond and leaves joints that never reach full strength.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

