        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Polymeric sand punishes you on a delay. Damp joints, a shallow fill, sand left on the paver faces or a heavy hand with the hose all look perfectly acceptable on installation day and reveal themselves weeks later as haze, crumbling joints or a patio that empties in the first storm.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Two steps deserve disproportionate care because their failures are the ones you cannot undo. Clear every trace of sand off the paver surface before watering — broom, then a leaf blower at a shallow angle — and watch the runoff while you water, stopping the moment it turns milky. Joint failures can be dug out and redone; haze bonded to the stone largely cannot.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then leave it alone. Twenty-four hours before walking on it, 24 to 48 before driving on it, and at least 30 days before any sealer touches it. Sealing over sand that is still releasing moisture is how a careful installation ends up hazy across its entire surface.
          </p>
        </div>


